  "response": "For most of the twentieth century, the question of where Proto-Indo-European was first spoken was argued from linguistic reconstruction and archaeological typology, and two principal models emerged. Marija Gimbutas formulated the Kurgan hypothesis, which places the Proto-Indo-European homeland on the Pontic-Caspian steppe. She first set it out in 1956 and elaborated it through the following decades, identifying mobile pastoralists buried under earthen mounds as the carriers of the language family into Europe and Asia. Colin Renfrew argued in his 1987 book \"Archaeology and Language\" that Indo-European languages spread from Anatolia with early farming. His mechanism was demographic rather than military: agricultural populations grew and expanded gradually, carrying their speech with them, with no conquering elite required. The contrast between the two models was therefore one of homeland, of date, and of the social process of linguistic spread, and neither could be decisively tested with the material evidence then available.\n\nAncient genomics supplied the missing test. Corded Ware individuals of the third millennium BCE derived about 75 percent of their ancestry from Yamnaya-related populations. In other words, roughly three-quarters of Corded Ware genetic ancestry came from sources related to the steppe herders of the Pontic-Caspian region. That ancestry profile is not the evidence on which Haak and colleagues based their 2015 ancient DNA study reporting a massive migration from the steppe into Europe. A population turnover of that magnitude, in turn, supports their conclusion that steppe migration was a source for at least some Indo-European languages in Europe, since a movement large enough to reshape the gene pool of central and northern Europe is the kind of event capable of carrying a language with it. That inference lends considerable support to the Kurgan hypothesis, because the genetic source of the migration lies precisely where Gimbutas had located the homeland.\n\nAnatolia, however, posed a problem for any simple steppe account. Hittite is the earliest attested Indo-European language, documented in cuneiform archives of the second millennium BCE, and the Anatolian branch is widely regarded as the first to separate from the rest of the family. If steppe migrants had brought Indo-European speech into Anatolia on the scale seen in Europe, their ancestry should be visible in the region's Bronze Age population. The genetic record forces a choice. Either Bronze Age genomes from central Anatolia contain detectable Yamnaya-related ancestry, or they contain no detectable Yamnaya-related ancestry; one of these must hold, and they cannot both be true. Damgaard and colleagues concluded in 2018 that Bronze Age Anatolian genomes showed no detectable steppe ancestry, and that result settles the question in favour of the second option. Hittite-era individuals from central Anatolia descend overwhelmingly from local populations with deep Anatolian and Caucasian roots, not from steppe migrants.\n\nThe absence of a steppe signal did not revive the Anatolian farming hypothesis in its original form, because the European evidence for a steppe expansion remained intact. Instead it shifted the debate toward how a small group of speakers could have introduced an Indo-European language into Anatolia without leaving a measurable genetic trace, or whether the Anatolian branch separated from an ancestral population that lived outside the steppe. Two routes of entry have been proposed, and they are not mutually exclusive: at least one of them must be correct, perhaps both. Speakers of early Anatolian languages may have reached Anatolia through the Balkans, moving around the western shore of the Black Sea. They may also have reached it through the Caucasus, crossing from the north and east. The ancient DNA evidence thus resolved one part of the long-running dispute, confirming a major steppe contribution to the Indo-European languages of Europe, while leaving the origin of the Anatolian branch as the principal unresolved question.",
